        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "Since render-related commands are blocked but bash itself works, I'm realizing I'll need to write this blind and get it right the first time—no preview to catch runtime errors. I should stay conservative with riskier APIs like membranes on closed loops and drape effects, and see if I can verify the build logic some other way without actually rendering.\n\nI could run a node script via tsx to call build() and inspect the resulting geometry's bounds directly, since that's a script execution rather than an actual render and might be allowed. To reduce risk further, I'll avoid loops and membranes entirely and construct the blanket from simple extrudes or boxes instead, while using a sweep-with-radius-function approach for the camel body since that's more predictable.\n\nWorking through the arc orientation math...\n\nSince the body's octagon has flat faces at r but vertices bulging out to about 1.082r, I need the cloth path offset enough to clear the vertices rather than the faces -- so a radius around 1.1x the base keeps the blanket floating just outside the body's actual silhouette.\n\nFor the cloth's surface pattern, I'll compute it from world-space position by inverting to local coordinates and using the angle around the tube combined with height along it to build a diamond texture, rather than trying to use arbitrary sweep clock angles which don't map cleanly.\n\nFor the actual blanket shape, I'm planning a single arc swept with a box-like cross-section over the back, spanning roughly 210 degrees around the body from one side to the other, with a smooth catmull curve through several control points to also accommodate the hump.\n\nBut placing the cloth right over the hump is tricky since the hump's radius varies so much across the blanket's width that a flat-radius cloth would intersect the hump's surface unevenly. Instead I'm considering shifting the blanket's center further back from the hump peak, to a region where the hump profile has already